  "content": "{\n  \"title\": \"Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI)-enabled logistics infrastructure integration framework in the greater bay area\",\n  \"authors\": [\"Hang Wu\", \"Linhao Huang\", \"Ming Li\", \"George Q. Huang\"],\n  \"publication\": \"Advanced Engineering Informatics 60 (2024) 102551\",\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"abstract\": \"The paper proposes a Cyber-Physical Internet (CPI) framework to facilitate logistics infrastructure integration in the Greater Bay Area (GBA) of China. The authors address challenges in regional infrastructure integration through a bottom-up approach based on computer network concepts. The proposed CPI-LI2 (logistics infrastructure integration) framework has two layers: a link layer for infrastructure fusion and a transport layer for routing mechanisms. The framework was validated through a simulation study using medical logistics data from the GBA, demonstrating its effectiveness for large-scale logistics networks with dynamic links.\",\n    \"introduction\": \"The introduction discusses how digitalization is transforming the logistics industry, particularly in the GBA where e-commerce growth has increased logistics demand despite limited land for new infrastructure. The authors note that the National 14th 5-Year Plan emphasizes logistics infrastructure integration as key for the GBA's development. They explain how CPI, which evolved from Physical Internet (PI), can facilitate infrastructure resource sharing through cyber-level information flows. The authors identify research gaps in existing CPI studies, including their top-down approach and limitations in handling large-scale network integration problems, and propose exploring computer network concepts to address these challenges.\",\n    \"literature_review\": \"The literature review covers four main areas: PI theory, logistics infrastructure in PI, routing in PI, and CPI frameworks. It traces the evolution from Physical Internet to Cyber-Physical Internet, discussing standardized containers, node types, and platforms. For routing, the review highlights various approaches including VRP models and routing protocols. Regarding CPI frameworks, it examines layered structures from different studies and identifies gaps in infrastructure interconnectivity and real-time large-scale network management that the current research aims to address.\",\n    \"general_framework\": \"The paper presents a two-layer CPI-LI2 framework with a CPI link layer and a CPI transport layer. The link layer divides logistics networks into autonomous areas, elects key areas and regional link centers, and maintains links. It includes services for data sources, clustering, elections, CPI generation, visualization, maintenance, and data warehousing. The transport layer makes routing decisions using services for routing table generation, link state evaluation, process traceability, and visualization. Together, these layers integrate the logistics network infrastructure and determine PI container flows.\",\n    \"cpi_link_layer_fusion_mechanism\": \"This section explains how the CPI link layer fusion works in three steps: pre-processing data from information systems and official websites, clustering spatial data to divide regions into autonomous areas, and electing core areas and regional link centers based on logistics development. The authors discuss clustering algorithms and assessment metrics (SSE, silhouette coefficient, CH index) to determine the optimal number of autonomous areas, balancing infrastructure integration, management, database size, and network security.\",\n    \"cpi_transport_layer_fusion_mechanism\": \"The transport layer section details the routing process for PI containers between autonomous areas using edge nodes and core nodes. It introduces two path types: Distance Vector Master Paths (DVMP) and Link State Branch Paths (LSBP). The CPI routing mechanism evaluates path status, selects healthy paths with lowest overhead, and updates routing tables in real-time. Different charging methods are described for master paths (distance-based) and branch paths (quantity-based ladder pricing).\",\n    \"case_study\": \"A case study applies the CPI-LI2 framework to the medical logistics industry in the GBA. Medical institutions and logistics hubs serve as PI demand nodes and PI logistics nodes. The experiment follows three steps: constructing the logistics network according to the link layer fusion mechanism, establishing routing tables per the transport layer fusion mechanism, and comparing the proposed CPI routing mechanism with Genetic Algorithm (GA) and Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO) across networks of different sizes. Results show the CPI routing mechanism outperforms GA and PSO in solution time and routing overhead, with advantages increasing with network size.\",\n    \"discussion\": \"The discussion interprets the case study results, noting that the CPI-LI2 framework successfully integrates logistics infrastructure in the GBA medical logistics network. It highlights how the framework enables independent service scopes for logistics nodes, reduces routing overhead, and maintains solution stability. The authors emphasize that the framework promotes resource sharing, balances infrastructure utilization, enhances interoperability between networks, and enables real-time response to disruptions.\",\n    \"conclusion\": \"The conclusion summarizes the framework's contributions to logistics infrastructure integration in the GBA. The two-layer CPI-LI2 framework divides regions into autonomous areas and establishes connections between them, while employing a combination of DVMP and LSBP routing mechanisms to coordinate transport resources. The authors note the framework's potential to improve interoperability through infrastructure integration and enable real-time monitoring and decision-making. They also acknowledge limitations in their approach and suggest future work on multimodal transport scenarios.\"\n  }\n}",
